1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,919
Friday deploy of the tolling-adjacent
reporting service. Same JAR that
developers ran against H2 on laptops.
Staging expected Postgres. Someone forgot
the active profile.

2
00:00:11,039 --> 00:00:18,399
The app started, Auto-DDL touched the
wrong assumptions, and the first health
check looked "up" while queries failed
with dialect errors.

3
00:00:18,519 --> 00:00:27,280
Three environments — local H2, staging
Postgres, prod Postgres with read replicas
— one artifact.

4
00:00:27,399 --> 00:00:40,879
Profiles exist so bean definitions and
properties can differ without forking the
codebase into reporting-local and
reporting-prod repositories. A profile is
a named gate on definitions and property
sources.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:57,960
Activate with spring.profiles.active, the
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE environment
variable, or programmatic API on the
context. Beans annotated @Profile("…") or
XML profile= attributes register only when
that profile is on.

6
00:00:58,079 --> 00:01:13,239
@Profile("!prod") excludes production.
Multiple profiles can be active at once;
the set is a bag, not a single enum,
unless ops enforces one-at-a-time in the
Deployment. Look at this java for a
moment. Walk the gates.

7
00:01:13,359 --> 00:01:24,079
@Profile("local") on localDataSource means
that bean definition is registered only
when local is active — H2 plus schema-
local.sql.

8
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:40,000
@Profile({"staging", "prod"}) on
postgresDataSource registers the same
factory method for either staging or prod;
JDBC URL still comes from Environment so
the two envs differ by property, not by a
third method.

9
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:52,080
@Profile("prod") on routingDataSource adds
a read/write splitter only in production:
it injects the postgres bean by qualifier
and builds a replica pool from
app.datasource.replica-url.

10
00:01:52,200 --> 00:02:02,320
In staging you get a single Postgres
DataSource; in prod you get routing on
top. Runtime with
spring.profiles.active=prod.

11
00:02:02,439 --> 00:02:14,079
Spring prepares the Environment, marks
prod active, and while parsing
configuration classes only accepts @Bean
methods whose profiles match.
localDataSource never enters the registry.

12
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:27,759
postgresDataSource and routingDataSource
do. If something @Autowired DataSource
without a qualifier in prod, you still
need clarity — often mark the routing bean
@Primary so repositories get the splitter.

13
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:34,479
Wrong or missing profile changes which
definitions exist: unsatisfied
dependencies can fail fast,

14
00:02:34,599 --> 00:02:44,560
or — worse — a leftover local in
spring.profiles.active=local,prod can
register H2 *and* Postgres and then lose
on primary selection.

15
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:52,520
Step through a staging boot with
spring.profiles.active=staging once more,
slower. Environment marks only staging.

16
00:02:52,640 --> 00:03:02,920
PersistenceProfiles is parsed;
localDataSource's @Profile("local") does
not match, so that definition is skipped
entirely — not created-and-disabled,
skipped.

17
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:17,280
postgresDataSource matches the
staging/prod set and registers.
routingDataSource requires prod, so
staging never builds the replica pool.
Repositories that @Autowired DataSource
receive the single Postgres bean.

18
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:21,320
First query uses the staging JDBC URL from
Environment.

19
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:34,600
If ops meant prod and typed production
instead of prod, none of the postgres
profile annotations match either — and if
nothing else defines a DataSource, refresh
fails with NoSuchBeanDefinitionException
for DataSource,

20
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:37,760
which is painful but clearer than talking
to the wrong cluster.

21
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:43,680
Failure mode from that Friday:
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE unset in the
staging Deployment.

22
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:51,800
Only default property sources load; if a
default DataSource auto-config or an
unprofiled bean still builds something,
startup may succeed.

23
00:03:51,920 --> 00:04:04,000
Symptom: SQLGrammarException or wrong
dialect messages on first query; H2 file
in /tmp on a staging pod;
Environment.getActiveProfiles() empty in
an actuator env dump.

24
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:15,360
Another failure: application-prod.yml
present in the JAR but profile never
activated — people assume the filename
alone selects prod. It does not;
activation is mandatory. Trade-offs.

25
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:24,439
Profiles keep one artifact and branch bean
graphs cleanly; they also hide behavior
behind an env var that must be set
correctly in every cluster.

26
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:32,759
Too many fine-grained profiles (local,
local-h2, local-postgres, qa1…) explode
combinatorial testing.

27
00:04:32,879 --> 00:04:41,519
Prefer coarse profiles for infrastructure
(local/staging/prod) and property overlays
for small differences (URLs, pool sizes).

28
00:04:41,639 --> 00:04:52,519
Profile-specific *beans* for incompatible
implementations (H2 vs Postgres); profile-
specific *properties* for the same bean
shape with different values.

29
00:04:52,639 --> 00:05:03,079
Misconception unique to profiles: "Setting
spring.profiles.active=prod replaces
application.properties entirely with
application-prod.properties." No.

30
00:05:03,199 --> 00:05:19,439
Profile-specific documents add and
override; the default
application.properties (or .yml) still
loads. Forgetting that is how a shared
default URL leaks into prod beside your
overrides. Profiles select which beans
exist.

31
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:36,040
The tolling ops team still needs a single
API to ask "what is the feature flag value
and which DB URL won after env vars,
files, and CLI flags fought?" That unified
view is the Environment abstraction.
